triage: fix_tests: The game matches the rules: after round 1, hand_number is 1, each seat holds 2 cards, the deck holds 18, and the trump card and leader are correct. The scenarios fail only because they script the bid as a menu option ('1' or '0'). R3/R4 make the bid an open numeric answer, so it is never offered as a list of options.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: The game deals, turns trump, validates the play and awards the Wizard trick exactly as the rules require. The trace shows the bid poll was held in round 1 (r1 seats 1-3 answered 0). The failing scenarios all stem from the tests matching the bid option by the question wording 'How many tricks will you take this hand?', which is not one of the options offered (the options are the bid values). So no answer rule ever matched and the default 'first' answered 0.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_game: Every failing scenario breaks on the same point: the bidding question never offered the options [0, 1, 2, 3], so the scripted bids (for example 2, 3 and 0) were never delivered and every bid stayed at 0. The rules frame the bid as a simultaneous answer of 0, 1, 2 or 3, which the platform supports as a simultaneous choice, so the file should present it that way.

